  "query_preview": "..Ensemble models have reached greater consensus on the\ntrack of the main system for next week. A weak upper-level trough\nis expected to move over the area Monday and Tuesday, bringing\nlight, and generally unimpactful precipitation. A mid-level low\nwill supply vorticity to the region Monday and Tuesday, amplifying\nanticipated rain showers before tapering off as high pressure\nbuilds on Wednesday. A significant upper-level ridge will build on\nWednesday, while the low-level ridge strengthens early next week\nas the low tracks south and east toward the AOR. High pressure\nremains the dominant feature, keeping the main low to the west as\nit tracks over the Aleutians, digs south, and moves into the Kenai\nPeninsula. While the current expectation is for the main frontal\nprecipitation to push through on Tuesday with a mean 24 hour\naccumulation of 0.25\u201d (supported by the 75th percentile NBM and\nGEFS/EC ensemble agreement), there remains a risk that if the low\nshifts further east, Yakutat and surrounding areas could\nexperience heavier bands of rain showers between Thursday and\nFriday. For now, the expected track will keep the Northeast Gulf\nCoast under light rain showers, but nothing unseasonable. After\nthe midweek system pushes through, the central and southern\npanhandle are expected to see clearer skies, and therefore warmer\ntemperatures. Current thinking puts inland and southern areas with\nhighs in the low 70s for Wednesday and Thursday.",
